<Slide variant="statement">

# The inverted pyramid

</Slide>

<Slide>

<InvertedPyramid />

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 1 of 4</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <PenLine aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>The lead</h1>
</div>

The opening of the story. A direct lead states the finding right away, like a bulletin. A delayed lead starts with a person or scene and arrives at the finding later.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>“On a spring afternoon in 2014, Brisha Borden was running late to pick up her god-sister from school when she spotted an unlocked kid’s blue Huffy bicycle and a silver Razor scooter.”</p>
  <p class="step-example-source">“Machine Bias” · ProPublica · May 23, 2016</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 2 of 4</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <Nut aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>The nut graf</h1>
</div>

The paragraph that tells readers why the story matters and what it will explain.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>“Scores like this — known as risk assessments — are increasingly common in courtrooms across the nation.”</p>
  <p class="step-example-source">“Machine Bias” · ProPublica · May 23, 2016</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 3 of 4</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <ChartColumn aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>The findings</h1>
</div>

The specific, newsworthy pattern in the data. A comparison gives the number meaning.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>“The score proved remarkably unreliable in forecasting violent crime: Only 20 percent of the people predicted to commit violent crimes actually went on to do so.”</p>
  <p class="step-example-source">“Machine Bias” · ProPublica · May 23, 2016</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 4 of 4</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <TriangleAlert aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>The caveats</h1>
</div>

The honest limits of the data, stated plainly. What the analysis cannot show, and what it leaves out.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>“[Northpointe] does not publicly disclose the calculations used to arrive at defendants’ risk scores, so it is not possible for either defendants or the public to see what might be driving the disparity.”</p>
  <p class="step-example-source">“Machine Bias” · ProPublica · May 23, 2016</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide variant="statement">

# Now let’s find all four in a real story.

## Doug Smith and Ben Welsh · Los Angeles Times · Dec. 18, 2018

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 1 · Lead</p>

# Delayed

> “Minutes after fire broke out in Woolsey Canyon, a community 12 miles to the south went into action.”

The story gives readers a scene first. The finding arrives four paragraphs later.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 2 · Nut graf</p>

> “A Times analysis of wildfire hazard across California found that hundreds of communities from Redding to San Diego are at high risk of deadly wildfires like those in Paradise and Malibu last month.”

## The stakes

“The findings follow a fire season of unprecedented destruction — nearly 20,000 homes lost, more than 100 people killed.”

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 3 · The finding</p>

# More than 1.1 million structures

## Roughly 1 in 10 buildings in California

lie within the highest-risk fire zones.

Los Angeles tops the list with at least 114,000 structures; San Diego is second with more than 88,000.

<Citation href="https://web.archive.org/web/20181218194516/https://www.latimes.com/projects/la-me-california-buildings-in-fire-zones/">Story map: “At least 1.1 million California buildings are in a very severe hazard zone” · archived Los Angeles Times story</Citation>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Element 4 · Caveats</p>

# The total is probably higher.

> “The Microsoft database of building footprints employed in the analysis, among the most complete lists available, is not comprehensive.”

The analysis also excludes portions of the state under federal responsibility.

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Showing your work</p>

# Make the method inspectable.

The story’s methodology note names Cal Fire hazard maps, Census Bureau place boundaries and Microsoft’s building-footprint database.

<Citation href="https://github.com/datadesk/california-fire-zone-analysis">Open-source analysis · github.com/datadesk/california-fire-zone-analysis</Citation>

</Slide>

